  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ can be used to transform the equation \n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & ={z}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-{z}^{-1} \\\\ -x{z}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-{z}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}.$",
